    "output": "# Leisure Activity Access: Gender Differences in Older Adults\n\n**Societal Norms:**\nOlder women often face greater social restrictions due to traditional gender expectations around caregiving responsibilities (grandchildren, spouses), which limit discretionary time. Safety concerns and transportation dependency may also restrict independent participation in activities, whereas older men traditionally have greater social acceptance for solo activities and social outings.\n\n**Financial Constraints:**\nWomen typically have lower retirement incomes due to interrupted work histories, caregiving gaps, and wage disparities during their working years, directly limiting access to paid leisure activities. Men generally accumulate more personal savings and pension benefits, providing greater financial flexibility for hobbies, travel, and recreational pursuits.\n\n**Combined Impact:**\nThese factors intersect to create compounded barriers for older women—even when financially able, social norms may discourage participation, and financial limitations often mean choosing between necessities and leisure. Conversely, older men benefit from both greater financial resources and culturally-sanctioned autonomy in leisure pursuits.",
